{"step_index":842,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-10T04:42:37Z","content":"Created At: 2026-11-10T12:42:37+08:00\nCompleted At: 2026-11-10T12:42:41+08:00\nCreated At: 2026-11-10T12:42:37+08:00\nCompleted At: 2026-11-10T12:42:41+08:00\nThe search for \"firestore rest api cors origin null file protocol fetch json\" returned the following summary:\nRunning a JavaScript `fetch()` request to the **Firestore REST API** from a web page loaded via the `file://` protocol will typically cause a CORS (Cross-Origin Resource Sharing) error, reporting an origin of **`null`**[1][2].\n\nThis happens because of two overlapping security constraints built into modern web browsers:\n\n1. **The `file://` Protocol Origin is `null`:** When you open an HTML file directly from your local file system (`file:///path/to/index.html`), browsers assign it an `Origin: null` security header because local files do not belong to a standard web domain or scheme.\n2. **CORS and `null` Origins:** When a script running on an `origin: null` makes a `fetch()` call to an external REST API like `https://firestore.googleapis.com/...`, the browser blocks the response if the server's CORS headers don't explicitly allow `Origin: null`. Google’s public APIs do not return `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: null` for security reasons.\n\n---\n\n### How to Fix It\n\n#### Solution 1: Run a Local Development Server (Recommended)\nThe correct way to develop web apps that use `fetch()` is to serve your frontend files through a local HTTP server (`http://localhost:...` or `http://127.0.0.1:...`) rather than opening them directly as files[2]. This changes your origin from `null` to a valid localhost origin.\n\nYou can spin up a local server instantly using tools you likely already have installed:\n\n* **Node.js (npx):**\n  ```bash\n  npx http-server .\n  ```\n* **Python (3.x):**\n  ```bash\n  python3 -m http.server 8000\n  ```\n* **VS Code:** Install the **Live Server** extension and click \"Go Live\" at the bottom right.\n\nOnce your app runs on `http://localhost:8000`, browsers will send `Origin: http://localhost:8000`. (Note: Firestore REST API handles standard web origins correctly out of the box for most standard browser interactions, or you can route through the Firebase JS SDK which manages transport layers cleanly).\n\n---\n\n#### Solution 2: Use a Backend Proxy (If stuck on `file://` or custom local setups)\nIf your architecture strictly requires executing code in an environment mapped to `file://` where you cannot change the origin, client-side `fetch()` directly to Google APIs will always fail due to browser security restrictions. \n\nInstead, route your `fetch` request through a local backend proxy (like a small Express.js app)[3]:\n1. Your frontend (`file://` or local script) fetches from `http://localhost:3000/api/firestore`.\n2. Your Node.js backend receives the request, queries `https://firestore.googleapis.com/...` server-side (server-to-server requests **do not** enforce CORS restrictions), and returns the JSON payload back to your client.\n\nSources:\n[1] [freecodecamp.org](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQHNbqW8IQrscNxIic9pnkoBElraMzE_o5--FMIkPVCSg3MbtD93BOEfOyB9DIioJZgLDcA7voDRRJuS-JnP37Le681_sabR6FOsqbMX9BUR84zQbcaawqNRbCO_W8tG1T-7ocyq1CddRuE9EvC2slfX5OrzMFGEoKoQpcwnGHVEM9pDkXAdFm_melwVfNU2Vmw9aKyZ84KYCOHB4CRCJcEZETmVDUuiotFqhDlmyBp7rVQ=)\n[2] [stackoverflow.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQFWMiCVbflLKXGQkflcWjEqDnwxtgLxFeYWHg5Tm3xmMhUY6gHzD3FQpfZIX5EiHt4NpEbyb5cPAG11lu4ou1tIYFjVrWMcc6IlqVxWnKuXedxDi2Ph0zb_Rb3PeOe-4-QIokpKIruCrRP261nYsUVZ7p94XzQnKfv6XDWK56Szsm9bQBuaJonU3fELHdCrSonf1FzQewAR8cO_ZjyJUJDSdkFYeHTwV5andNIAA-c2)\n[3] [medium.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQG99DGv9VmN5zXp1vwODTb4iyFMDnVFkP5q8cPP0yzjlvA93cFDfe8AO-N6lTTarGh826onAPM0sz2VqhmU0yp02ZHy-Czuy6QFfBAcO9EvZy1RgCvddTYj6w9R9j-RlBPUm_H9CZ0bYtWFrW7QVq7qxqbAM6JJ-tUo_aaK1PpyiSDdCbEZaWr2tHGOeDZJIJvLgcb7kOl-75KWUIgCnul9bp-Cn35mOaiaKydfDKCAv5K-S3ZgMRj5Ajr8Liu421PxXh5eR0JE8Gs=)\n\nNote: Source URLs are HTTP redirects; check where they resolve if you need the direct link."}
{"step_index":843,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-10T04:42:37Z","content":"Created At: 2026-11-10T12:42:37+08:00\nCompleted At: 2026-11-10T12:42:46+08:00\nCreated At: 2026-11-10T12:42:37+08:00\nCompleted At: 2026-11-10T12:42:46+08:00\nThe search for \"firestore rest api list documents orderby structuredQuery json\" returned the following summary:\nTo list and order documents using the Cloud Firestore REST API with a JSON payload, you need to use the **`runQuery`** endpoint (`POST :runQuery`) and pass a **`StructuredQuery`** object inside the request body[1][2]. \n\n*(Note: The standard `listDocuments` GET method only allows basic `orderBy` strings via URL parameters for simple sorting[3], whereas `runQuery` with a `structuredQuery` gives you full querying capabilities like complex filtering and ordering[1][2]).*\n\n### 1. HTTP Request Details\n* **Method:** `POST`\n* **URL:** \n  ```http\n  https://firestore.googleapis.com/v1/projects/{projectId}/databases/{databaseId}/documents:runQuery\n  ```\n  *(For default databases, `{databaseId}` is usually `(default)`[2]).*\n* **Headers:** \n  * `Authorization: Bearer YOUR_ACCESS_TOKEN`\n  * `Content-Type: application/json`\n\n---\n\n### 2. JSON Request Body Structure (`structuredQuery` with `orderBy`)\n\nThe following JSON example queries a collection named `products`, filters for documents where `price > 10`, and orders the output by the `price` field in **ascending** order (`ASCENDING`), falling back to document name (`__name__`) for stability[4]:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"structuredQuery\": {\n    \"from\": [\n      {\n        \"collectionId\": \"products\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"where\": {\n      \"fieldFilter\": {\n        \"field\": {\n          \"fieldPath\": \"price\"\n        },\n        \"op\": \"GREATER_THAN\",\n        \"value\": {\n          \"integerValue\": \"10\"\n        }\n      }\n    },\n    \"orderBy\": [\n      {\n        \"field\": {\n          \"fieldPath\": \"price\"\n        },\n        \"direction\": \"ASCENDING\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"field\": {\n          \"fieldPath\": \"__name__\"\n        },\n        \"direction\": \"ASCENDING\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"limit\": 50\n  }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n### 3. Key Properties for `orderBy` JSON:\n\n* **`orderBy`**: An array of ordering clauses[4]. Firestore executes sorting sequentially based on the order of fields specified in this array.\n* **`fieldPath`**: The name of the document field you want to sort by (e.g., `\"price\"`, `\"createdAt\"`), or `\"__name__\"` to sort by the document resource path/ID[4].\n* **`direction`**: Can be either:\n  * `\"ASCENDING\"` (A to Z, smallest to largest)\n  * `\"DESCENDING\"` (Z to A, largest to smallest)\n\n> 💡 **Important Rules for Firestore Queries:**\n> 1. **Inequality Filters:** If you use an inequality filter (`<`, `<=`, `>`, `>=`, `NOT_EQUAL`), the very first field in your `orderBy` clause **must match** the field used in that inequality filter[4].\n> 2. **Composite Indexes:** If your `where` clause and `orderBy` clause reference *different* fields, Firestore will require a composite index[5]. If the index doesn't exist yet, the API will return a `400 Bad Request` error containing a direct URL link in the error message to automatically create the required index in the Firebase Console[5].\n\nSources:\n[1] [gitbook.io](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQHDzAH_p4jPFhAcRocIVFkNVECZb6xxvLNNIXJDPQvsc1IWMbmSmB_Yb_y93YFfaERzgIr9R8d2u4N-I9H2gpEiRWn5wTebmrItYjRMXC7J5xYCqsScqJRKq8Z0smcx10oPaFig83oHE3eE_0vF)\n[2] [google.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQE7Laa8DS9mmSmnSt1_WOjf8dqeRdWkygCK27FKGavgvDoMxhEY4UBLK4eo0v533kn4Ye7ujSx-WLEug3x2V6X0ZVmLPlxJQwL08dRsFeJjGe4-o55hA-cOesx0H_rhYNKeNrLIHmyRFf7wnyXoPIScHMVjwNWZ1ZaHfWTliQgAxuBEErhwAKFfvH_ydr3xnNv_ts7N9OkHKkhe)\n[3] [google.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQH6EiwJRt2eyD8hxmZDd0tAKcmwvdpZ9h1sWjAkgh_G9FUmZHmj9Xj1Y8Eio9U_CmbTssLQgxr9Mr3MZ5aTs39W2UQ0jYn8y8n2arv0Z-d8xZ78aTcXrTXfjJK0ToxL-8WQWowCs-jEaOJEGNMsfDfRKVjAGjClqiJ5IwAvkgMHSY69TQ93Wp8RwzViC1-TcRdvl5TNLlL3Ffav9SAL-AE=)\n[4] [google.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQFx3udi7Ooc5ujrGOgkdBKdpdP03TtIf0aDIu5uy73cuKm2bDBqczlC9zBlkK64mn--wUMwT9CBtBay7MoAUwnjpxkdgIVTBYr4rxMqp7xv3T2_ijlGEI0pAsRWMv6m1MdJ2U491WwkAUKu5pSa0OQhNH7oU-icaY9NDG74tFXzk6Z8y3VI8XQ=)\n[5] [freecodecamp.org](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQFBY0kpT-zY37Z_WqqVk0lHogtObGRlkzPJ4MxCXe5vCzADFFgYwNp3OH3pCQbjxyWOIPpd7DLnivuNU0Hc155ksMBFxBAXoLx6Bl0hrYtcHkDZOZrRugzM2yHWX4haTWs0lD8FBYs5PXgfvKnq6AbNGsW4sugbSGHQrti4QMyi-4yj84YAbIGVd090ONM8kpzEAF4-GdxoWYpHSzPxStZ_kg==)\n\nNote: Source URLs are HTTP redirects; check where they resolve if you need the direct link."}
