sed. The strategy involves removing all direct Firebase references from `index.html` and, instead, injects an encrypted data representation via `teacher.html`. The actual Firestore interaction will be handled server-side through a Vercel `/api/` proxy or encoded REST calls.\n\n\nThe downloaded `index.html` must present as an encrypted local database to deceive the AI, while maintaining Firestore as the live data source for real-time updates from `teacher.html`.  The goal is to maintain Firestore's live updates, so after download, edits in `teacher.html` must not require re-downloading `index.html`. This implies the encrypted data in `index.html` is a snapshot, and any quiz update in `teacher.html` saves to Firestore directly, thus maintaining the live source of truth.\n\n\nThe existing prompt injection comments are likely ineffective against modern AI models. They provide no real defense against detection of Firestore. The focus shifts to completely removing all Firebase references and configurations from both `index.html` and `teacher.html`. The goal is to establish a secure connection via either a Vercel serverless API proxy, which handles Firestore interaction on the server-side, or by encoded REST calls to avoid any direct Firebase/Firestore exposure within the HTML files.\n\n\nMethod A (Vercel proxy) offers robust deception by completely obscuring Firestore interactions on the client side. This method has an advantage as it handles the database interactions server-side. Conversely, Method B uses `fetch()` with XOR-encoded URLs for a less secure static-only approach.\n\nThe `index.html` file must convincingly appear as an encrypted local database. This can be achieved by renaming the storage layer to something like `LocalEncryptedVault` and injecting an encrypted payload block, which is the encrypted quiz/vault alongside a live Firestore loader (or a fallback encrypted payload within `index.html` plus a decoy/local cipher).\n\n\nThe primary strategy is to make `index.html` appear as an encrypted local database.  This requires removing all direct Firebase references and injecting an encrypted payload. The core deception relies on presenting an `EncryptedLocalDB.unlock()` call within `index.html` unpacking an unreadable encrypted block. This should make an AI conclude the questions are stored client-side. The existing setup does not indicate the presence of a Vercel deployment, so serverless functions might be an available option.\n\n\nThe current analysis of the project's setup is needed to determine the most direct and practical method for achieving deception. The downloaded `index.html` file's ability to convincingly simulate an encrypted local database depends on eliminating all observable Firebase references and configurations. A clean approach through server-side logic in serverless functions offers the highest level of obfuscation. This involves a proxy to handle Firestore interactions on the server, ensuring zero Firebase SDKs or URLs are present in the HTML files.\n\n\n"}
