{"step_index":863,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-10T20:45:57Z","input_tokens":2810,"cache_read_tokens":284474,"output_tokens":3876,"thinking":"The goal is to design an architecture that maintains full Firestore compatibility for Approach B. This involves ensuring identical behavior for both Vercel and double-click access across all read and write operations on the `quizzes/active`, `quizzes/template`, and `submissions` collections, including commit operations. Verification will be crucial.\n\n\nThe current focus is on creating a robust architecture that maintains Firestore compatibility. The approach involves a clean separation from Firebase SDK script tags and plaintext identifiers. The `index.html` and `teacher.html` sources should feature a unified and compact `EncryptedLocalDatabase` module. This module will utilize XOR and a rolling PRNG/SHA-inspired stream key to store an encoded endpoint buffer.\n\n\nThe architecture incorporates a local vault within downloaded files, containing an encrypted quiz payload injected during download. This allows seamless fallback to the local version if Firestore access is unavailable while still syncing with the Firestore REST endpoint if the connection is available, and ensuring no Firebase dependencies or secrets are present in client-side code. The primary key to this approach is this offline capability with live Firestore syncing.\n\n\nThe design ensures all sensitive data is encrypted locally within downloaded files, employing a dedicated `EncryptedLocalDatabase` module. This module uses XOR encryption and a stream key derived locally to encode an endpoint buffer, ensuring no plaintext secrets or Firebase dependencies are exposed client-side. The system maintains live Firestore syncing, with the local vault serving as a fallback when access is unavailable.\n\n\n\nThe current architecture allows direct writes to Firestore, and initial loading in `index.html` remains dependent